e  past but we also need to be good  relations to one another so to work  against a sort of hierarchical models  and those power dynamics to recognize  and respect them to be more  collaborative in community oriented Even  in our how we run our lab how we work  with students and so for me this is a  very It's a Wonderful way to kind of  take some of the principles that I see  at the core of indigenous archeology but  also connect them in with other similar  movements right other similar ways that  people are thinking about archeology and  what it can and should be globally  right because it's this idea that we  want to create a space where any  archaeologist with a heart can practice  heart-centered archeology and that was  our invitation really in the book what  so so one more time heart is an acronym  for yeah uh heart-centered  I have to remember how we did it  um  RT I remember  heart centered and emotional  archeologies for research and teaching  h-e-a-r-t uh is the acronym and is this  Collective of women at this point it's  it's majority women who are really sort  of thinking through and feeling through  a lot of this and we've had an amazing  response to the book itself and to the  work of Heart Center practice because  especially a younger generation of  archaeologists from all sort of genders  and backgrounds I think have been  looking for an approach so even if their  interests are in in a very different  realm than say indigenous archeology  they want to do archeology differently  and they want to do archeology that  really matters that has a positive  contribution in the world today in a  very direct way so not just archeology  for the sake of knowing the history of  humanity but archeology for addressing  the history of colonization or for  telling different kinds of stories and I  see such an amazing generation of  archaeologists emerging who are going to  really lead us and all kinds of new and  amazing directions it gives me a lot of  Hope oh that's